in @prism

in @mirror/glass/ast
in @mirror/glass/ast/shape
in @mirror/glass/ast/token
in @epistemologic/property

# @mirror/glass/ast/shape/property — the `property` shape variant:
# a verdict-returning declaration that establishes a model-checked
# check on AST values.
#
# Per the per-variant file pattern declared in @mirror/glass/ast/
# shape: variants of `shape` may land in their own sub-file when
# they want to declare structure of their own. `property` does —
# it carries a name, optional args, and a body, with the disciplined
# constraint that the return type is always `verdict`.
#
# Substrate-altitude reading: a `property` is a function-shaped
# declaration whose return type is FIXED to `verdict` by
# construction. Compare with `lambda`, which carries a free
# `return` field; a `property` doesn't — that's what makes it a
# property and not a generic lambda.
#
# Legacy correspondence (corpus examples):
#
#   property autopoietic() -> verdict { ... }
#   property exhaustive(match_expr) -> verdict { ... }
#   property halts() -> verdict { ... }
#
# The `-> verdict` is redundant at the substrate altitude — every
# property returns a verdict by definition. The legacy form keeps
# the arrow for surface symmetry with `lambda`; at the substrate-
# altitude declaration, the return is structurally guaranteed.
#
# Verdict source:
#
#   verdict := pass | fail(diagnostic) | partial(f64, [diagnostic])
#
# Declared in @epistemologic/property; imported here so the field
# type resolves.
#
# Distinction from `lambda`:
#
#   - `lambda`   : free return type; may be impure (`io` flag); the
#                  general function-shaped declaration.
#   - `property` : return is fixed to `verdict`; pure by construction
#                  (no `io` flag); the model-checked dual of `lambda`.
#
# Both share name + args + body structure; the difference is the
# return-type discipline and the IO posture. Properties live in
# the verification altitude (@epistemologic/property/*); lambdas
# live in the implementation altitude.

grammar @mirror/glass/ast/shape/property {
  # The `property` shape variant: name + args + body. The return
  # type is structurally fixed to `verdict` and therefore does not
  # appear as a field — it's a property OF the variant, not OF an
  # instance. Args follow the lambda convention: name + type-name
  # identifier pairs. Body is a brace_block (opaque-to-substrate at
  # this altitude; downstream lifts).
  type property {
    name: identifier,
    args: [(identifier, identifier)],
    body: brace_block,
  }
}

out property
out @mirror/glass/ast/shape/property
